      "problem": "What are the solutions of the equation $(x+3)^{2}=-4?$",
      "worked_solution": "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-3+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-3-2\\,\\text{i}.$",
